from lessons1 import L, les
les(id="boot",t="Boot chain, U-Boot/ABL and secure boot",tag="Boot",
an="Booting is a relay race. The boot ROM (burned into the chip) runs first and hands the baton to a small loader, which hands it to U-Boot (or Qualcomm's ABL), which hands it to the kernel, which hands it to init. In secure boot, every runner checks the next runner's ID badge (signature) before passing the baton.",
j=[("Boot ROM","Code fixed in the SoC at manufacture; it can't be changed."),("SPL / XBL","A tiny first-stage loader that sets up DRAM. XBL is Qualcomm's."),("U-Boot / ABL","Full bootloader; ABL is Qualcomm's Android bootloader (based on UEFI/edk2)."),("Chain of trust","Each stage verifies the signature of the next before running it."),("AVB","Android Verified Boot: verifies boot/vendor/system partitions using vbmeta.")],
d="""<h4>Typical stages</h4><ol><li>ROM loads first stage from eMMC/UFS/SPI flash (fuses decide boot source and whether signatures are required).</li><li>SPL/XBL: clocks, PMIC, DDR training.</li><li>Trusted firmware (ARM TF-A, BL31) and optionally a TEE (OP-TEE / QTEE).</li><li>U-Boot or ABL: reads env, picks A/B slot, loads kernel + DTB (+ initramfs), passes cmdline.</li><li>Kernel: decompress, early console (<code>earlycon</code>), probe drivers, mount root.</li><li>init (systemd/busybox on Linux, Android <code>init</code>).</li></ol>
<h4>U-Boot skills to practice</h4><ul><li>Console: <code>printenv</code>, <code>setenv bootargs</code>, <code>mmc dev; mmc read</code>, <code>fdt addr; fdt print /soc</code>, <code>booti $kernel_addr - $fdt_addr</code>.</li><li>Board code + DT in U-Boot is separate from the kernel's: keep them in sync.</li><li>Fastboot in bootloader for flashing; <code>ums</code> to expose eMMC as USB mass storage.</li></ul>
<h4>Bring-up debugging</h4><p>No output? Check UART pins/baud first, then add <code>earlycon</code> and <code>keep_bootcon</code>, <code>initcall_debug</code>, and in a hang use JTAG to read the PC. 'Starting kernel ...' then silence is usually wrong DTB, wrong load address, or console not enabled in DT.</p>
<h4>Secure boot</h4><p>Public key hash blown into fuses → ROM verifies first stage → each stage verifies the next → AVB verifies Android partitions with dm-verity. Plus anti-rollback counters (see Lab 09). Never ship debug keys; keep signing in a controlled CI step, not on laptops.</p>""",
anim=("flow","Secure boot relay",["Boot ROM (fused key hash)","SPL / XBL: DDR init","TF-A BL31 + TEE","U-Boot / ABL: pick slot A/B","Linux kernel + DTB","init → services","AVB/dm-verity guards system"],["verifies SPL signature","verifies next image","secure monitor","verifies boot.img/vbmeta","earlycon, probe drivers","Android init or systemd","blocks tampered blocks at read time"]),
q=[("Kernel prints nothing after 'Starting kernel'. Three likely causes?","Wrong/missing DTB for the board, wrong load address/compression, console/UART not enabled or wrong console= in bootargs. Use earlycon to see more."),("What protects against flashing an old, signed but vulnerable image?","Anti-rollback: a version counter in fuses/RPMB that images must be ≥ to boot.")],lab="09-dfu-ab-image")

les(id="yocto",t="Yocto, Buildroot and living with vendor BSPs",tag="Build",
an="A BSP is a vendor's meal kit: everything in the box, pre-measured, but for their kitchen. Yocto is your recipe book that says 'take the kit, swap the sauce, add our spices' as layers, so you can repeat the same meal next year with a new kit.",
j=[("Yocto / BitBake","A build system that assembles a full Linux image from recipes (.bb) organized in layers."),("Layer (meta-*)","A folder of recipes and appends; e.g. meta-qcom, meta-freescale, meta-poly."),("bbappend","Modifies another layer's recipe without editing it."),("Buildroot","A simpler make-based image builder."),("Downstream / upstream","Upstream is the main kernel.org project; downstream is a vendor or company fork.")],
d="""<h4>Yocto day-to-day</h4><pre>source oe-init-build-env build
bitbake core-image-minimal           # or your product image
bitbake -c devshell linux-yourboard  # shell inside the kernel recipe's workdir
bitbake -c menuconfig virtual/kernel
devtool modify linux-yourboard       # edit source, then devtool finish to emit patches
bitbake -e myapp | grep ^SRC_URI=     # see final variable values</pre>
<p>Keep company changes in <code>meta-company</code> with <code>.bbappend</code> files and kernel <code>.cfg</code> fragments; never edit vendor layers in place. Pin layer revisions (kas/repo manifests) so builds are reproducible.</p>
<p><b>Try without hardware:</b> <code>MACHINE=qemuarm64 bitbake core-image-minimal &amp;&amp; runqemu qemuarm64 nographic</code> (needs ~50-100 GB disk and several hours the first time; not run on our box).</p>
<h4>Vendor BSP strategy (NXP, Qualcomm, Ambarella)</h4><ul><li>Track which vendor release you're on, and keep your patches as a numbered series on top (rebases become mechanical).</li><li>Before patching a vendor driver, search upstream: often the fix exists. Prefer backporting upstream commits (with <code>Fixes:</code> tags) to inventing your own.</li><li>File vendor tickets with a minimal repro, kernel version, register dumps, logs, and an ask. That is a senior skill that saves weeks.</li><li>Keep a 'BSP delta' document: what you changed, why, and whether it's upstreamable.</li></ul>""",
anim=("flow","How an image is assembled",["poky + vendor layers (meta-qcom/meta-freescale)","meta-company (bbappends, cfg fragments)","BitBake parses recipes","fetch → patch → configure → compile","package (ipk/rpm/deb)","rootfs + kernel + dtb → image (wic)","flash / runqemu"],["vendor BSP kit","your spices, no in-place edits","dependency graph","per recipe tasks, sstate cache","per-package outputs","sdcard/eMMC image","test it"]),
q=[("You need to change one kernel config option for the product. Where?","A .cfg fragment in your layer's linux bbappend, not by editing the vendor defconfig in place."),("Why keep vendor patches as a numbered series?","So moving to the next vendor BSP release is a rebase with clear conflicts, and each patch has a reason and upstream status.")],lab=None)

les(id="v4l2",t="Camera pipeline: V4L2, media controller, ALSA/ASoC audio",tag="Media",
an="V4L2 is a cafeteria tray system: you hand the kitchen (driver) empty trays (buffers), it fills them with frames and puts them on the counter, you take one, eat (process/encode), and hand it back. If you're slow to hand back trays, the kitchen drops frames.",
j=[("V4L2","Video4Linux2: the kernel API for cameras, encoders and HDMI capture (/dev/videoN)."),("Media controller","Describes the graph of sensor → CSI receiver → ISP → video node; configured with media-ctl."),("Subdevice","/dev/v4l-subdevN: a block inside the pipeline (sensor, bridge) with its own formats."),("ALSA / ASoC","Linux audio. ASoC splits drivers into codec, platform (DMA) and machine (board glue)."),("vivid","A virtual V4L2 test driver; lets you test camera code without a camera.")],
d="""<h4>Capture loop (Lab 07)</h4><p><code>QUERYCAP → S_FMT → REQBUFS → QUERYBUF+mmap → QBUF all → STREAMON → loop{DQBUF; use; QBUF} → STREAMOFF</code>. For zero copy into an encoder, export buffers with <code>VIDIOC_EXPBUF</code> (dma-buf fd) or use <code>V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF</code>.</p>
<h4>Conference-camera pipeline</h4><p>Sensor (MIPI CSI-2) → SoC CSI receiver → ISP (Qualcomm Spectra, Ambarella's ISP) → scaler → encoder (H.264/H.265) or straight to USB UVC (YUY2/NV12/MJPEG). HDMI-in uses a bridge (Lontium/Toshiba) as a V4L2 subdevice that reports timings (<code>VIDIOC_QUERY_DV_TIMINGS</code>) and EDID.</p>
<p>Useful commands: <code>v4l2-ctl --list-devices</code>, <code>--list-formats-ext</code>, <code>--stream-mmap --stream-count=100</code> (prints fps), <code>media-ctl -p</code>, <code>v4l2-compliance -d /dev/video0</code> (run it on every driver change).</p>
<h4>Audio</h4><p>Conferencing lives or dies on audio: echo cancellation needs <b>mic and speaker on the same clock</b> or with known drift. Watch for XRUNs (<code>aplay -v</code>, <code>/proc/asound/card*/pcm*/sub*/status</code>), period/buffer sizes, and sample-rate mismatches between USB UAC (host clock) and I2S codecs (local clock): asynchronous UAC with feedback endpoints exists for exactly this.</p>
<p>On Android, these sit under Camera HAL3 (<code>android.hardware.camera.provider</code>) and Audio HAL; same kernel APIs underneath.</p>""",
anim=("seq","V4L2 buffer ping-pong",["App","V4L2 core","Driver/DMA"],[(0,1,"REQBUFS 4, mmap"),(0,1,"QBUF #0..#3 (empty trays)"),(0,1,"STREAMON"),(2,2,"DMA fills #0, IRQ"),(2,1,"vb2_buffer_done(#0)"),(1,0,"DQBUF → #0 (full)"),(0,0,"encode / send over UVC"),(0,1,"QBUF #0 back")]),
q=[("Frame rate halves under load but driver has no errors. Where to look?","The app holds buffers too long (not QBUF'ing back), or too few buffers; check sequence gaps in DQBUF'd buffers."),("Why does UAC audio drift against a local I2S codec?","Different clocks: host USB SOF clock vs local crystal. Use async UAC with a feedback endpoint or resampling.")],lab="07-v4l2-capture")

les(id="codec",t="Video codecs (H.264/H.265/SVC) and HDMI on a conferencing device",tag="Media",
an="A codec is like describing a movie scene over the phone: send one full picture (I-frame), then only describe what moved (P/B-frames). SVC is sending the description in layers so a slow listener can stop after the first layer and still get the gist.",
j=[("I/P/B frames","I: self-contained; P: predicted from earlier; B: from earlier and later."),("GOP","Group of pictures: frames between I-frames."),("SVC","Scalable Video Coding: layered stream (temporal/spatial/quality) so receivers drop layers instead of the whole stream."),("EDID","Data a display sends describing its supported modes."),("HPD","Hot Plug Detect: HDMI pin signaling a display was connected."),("HDCP","HDMI content protection handshake.")],
d="""<h4>Why it matters below the framework</h4><ul><li>Hardware encoders are V4L2 M2M devices (stateful or stateless) or vendor APIs (Qualcomm/Ambarella SDKs). You tune bitrate control (CBR/VBR), GOP, IDR on demand (when a participant joins), slice size for packet loss, and latency (no B-frames for real-time).</li><li><b>SVC/simulcast</b>: video meetings send several layers; temporal layers let an SFU (selective forwarding server) drop frames per participant. Your firmware must expose layer controls and keep timestamps exact.</li><li>Measure glass-to-glass latency: camera exposure → ISP → encoder → USB/network. Budget each stage.</li></ul>
<h4>HDMI path (your Lontium experience)</h4><ul><li>HDMI-out: DSI/DPI from SoC → bridge (e.g. LT9611) → HDMI. Driver is a DRM bridge; EDID read over DDC (I2C); mode validation against bridge limits.</li><li>HDMI-in (laptop sharing): bridge → CSI-2 → V4L2. Handle source-change events (<code>V4L2_EVENT_SOURCE_CHANGE</code>), re-query timings, restart streaming.</li><li>Common bugs: EDID not re-read after hot-plug, HPD debounce, pixel clock out of range, color space (RGB vs YCbCr, limited vs full range) causing washed-out images, HDCP failures on certain TVs.</li></ul>""",
anim=("flow","Glass-to-glass latency budget",["Sensor exposure","ISP processing","Scaler / composition","H.264/H.265 encode","Packetize (RTP) / or UVC","Network / USB","Decode + display"],["~8-33 ms","few ms","few ms","1 frame or less (no B-frames)","slice-based helps","jitter buffer","receiver side"]),
q=[("A participant joins mid-call and sees gray for 5 s. Fix?","Send an IDR (keyframe) on request (PLI/FIR) instead of waiting for the next periodic I-frame."),("Image looks washed out on one TV only. Suspect?","RGB quantization range mismatch (limited vs full) or YCbCr/RGB choice from EDID/AVI infoframe.")],lab=None)
